Circuit Diagram Description
The circuit diagram illustrates the electrical connections of the Arduino-based Fire Fighting
Robot. The Arduino Uno acts as the central controller and is connected to a 5-channel
flame sensor module, an L298N motor driver module, and a relay module. The flame
sensor continuously monitors the surroundings and sends fire detection signals to the Arduino
through its digital output pins.
The Arduino processes these signals and controls the movement of the robot by sending
commands to the L298N motor driver, which drives the four DC motors connected to the
wheels. This enables the robot to move toward the detected fire source. The motor driver
receives power from the battery pack and supplies the required current to the motors.
A relay module is connected to the Arduino to control the water pump. When a flame is
detected, the Arduino activates the relay, which switches on the water pump. The pump then
sprays water through the nozzle to extinguish the fire. The entire system is powered by an
11.1V Li-ion battery pack, which provides power to the motor driver, water pump, and
control circuitry.
The circuit integrates sensing, motion control, and fire extinguishing functions, allowing the
robot to detect fire, navigate toward it, and automatically suppress it with minimal human
intervention
